    "word_count": 52,
    "html": "<p>Scott Storch is an American record producer who began as a keyboard player with the Roots. He moved into studio production, writing and producing hits for Beyoncé and 50 Cent. Melodies became his musical signature, while fees and royalties generated substantial earnings during the most commercially successful years of his recording career.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "The piano figure in Dr. Dre's Still D.R.E. emerged while Scott Storch was experimenting over a drum pattern in the studio. Dre had stepped into the adjoining kitchen, but remained within earshot. Hearing Storch's playing, he returned to select the idea; by evening, the track had gone to Jay-Z for lyrics.\n\nStorch also taught himself melodic patterns from Arabic music, using his keyboard's pitch wheel to imitate their inflections. His approach to recording preserved small variations in performance. He sometimes played a part through the song instead of repeating a short loop mechanically, allowing subtle changes to give it a more human feel.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Two direct interviews explain the studio event and distinct keyboard and recording methods."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 105,
    "html": "<p>The piano figure in Dr. Dre's Still D.R.E. emerged while Scott Storch was experimenting over a drum pattern in the studio. Dre had stepped into the adjoining kitchen, but remained within earshot. Hearing Storch's playing, he returned to select the idea; by evening, the track had gone to Jay-Z for lyrics.</p>\r\n<p>Storch also taught himself melodic patterns from Arabic music, using his keyboard's pitch wheel to imitate their inflections. His approach to recording preserved small variations in performance. He sometimes played a part through the song instead of repeating a short loop mechanically, allowing subtle changes to give it a more human feel.</p>\r\n"
  },
